#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""gs_wake_agent - the vault half. Boot, ask, do ONE job, power off.

Runs on the ThinkPad as a systemd oneshot at boot. It is the reason the vault
can be OFF for weeks: OPSEC_SETUP.md §5 step 8, "Idle weeks: ThinkPad is off.
Only the Pi hums."

    gs_wake_agent --key /etc/gs_wake_thinkpad.key

THE SHAPE OF THIS FILE IS THE POINT
`run_once()` returns a reason code and NEVER exits. `main()` wraps it in a
try/finally that calls `power_off()`. That is not tidiness, it is the whole
feature:

    fail-closed everywhere else in this repo means sys.exit()
    fail-closed HERE means the machine turns off

gs_common.verify_tor, tor_recheck, validate_proxy and newnym(required=True) all
abort with sys.exit. If this agent were written the ordinary house way, a Tor
failure would exit the process, systemd would mark the unit failed, and the
vault would sit powered on, on the LAN, with the disk auto-unlocked, until
somebody walked over. That is strictly worse than the row OPSEC_SETUP.md §6
scores for "they take the ThinkPad", and it happens on the four most ordinary
paths there are: no job pending, Tor not up yet, cable out, Pi rebooting.

`finally` runs on SystemExit too, so gs_common's aborts are covered without
touching gs_common.

POWER-OFF IS THREE INDEPENDENT ROOT-OWNED PATHS, and this file is only one:
  1. this agent's `finally`;
  2. `OnFailure=gs-wake-poweroff.service` on the unit, for a crash;
  3. `gs-wake-deadman.timer`, armed at boot by the same target that starts the
     agent and independent of it, for a SIGKILL or a hang.
None is conditional on the job succeeding. See systemd/ in this repo.

A BOOT THAT CANNOT DO THE JOB MUST NOT CONSUME THE JOB.
Every precondition is checked BEFORE M1 goes out. If Tor is down, if the spend
USB is in, if the disk is full, if the deadman is not armed -- the agent powers
off without ever asking, and the job is still pending at the Pi for the next
boot. Asking first and failing second would silently burn the operator's poke.

WHAT A JOB MAY BE
Whatever `gs_wake_proto.JOBS` says, and nothing else. The Pi sends a job id and
a closed set of typed scalars; this file composes 100% of the argv itself from
that table plus its own keyfile. No value from the note ever becomes a flag
name, a path, a URL or a proxy. See gs_wake_proto for the attack that rule
exists for.
"""
